\begin_layout Standard
Predicting football match outcomes is difficult because of the high levels of noise,
 changing team strategies,
 and unpredictable match events.
 Standard predictive models often struggle in these volatile environments.
 This research is important for three main reasons:
 First,
 it addresses the core issue of probability calibration in sports analytics.
 Machine learning models often produce raw scores that do not reflect real-world probabilities,
 especially when the data is noisy.
 By implementing methods like Isotonic Calibration and using the Newton–Raphson 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "power"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 approach to remove bookmaker margins,
 we provide a framework that transforms biased model outputs into reliable,
 objective probabilities.
 Second,
 we conduct a rigorous comparative analysis under strictly uniform conditions.
 Instead of focusing on just one algorithm,
 we test fundamentally different models—
including linear models,
 GAM 
