\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Leaves
\series default
:
 Defines the complexity of each tree by setting the maximum number of leaves.
 A higher value allows the model to capture more complex patterns but increases the risk of overfitting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Trees
\series default
:
 Specifies the total number of trees to build.
 Increasing this number generally improves model stability and predictive performance but requires more training time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
MinExample
\series default
:Sets the minimum number of data samples required to create a leaf.
 This acts as a regularization mechanism;
 higher values prevent the model from learning from noise or outliers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
LearnRate
\series default
:
 Controls the contribution of each tree to the final prediction.
 A lower learning rate makes the optimization process more robust,
 requiring more trees to reach an optimal solution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
FeatureFraction
\series default
:
 Determines the percentage of features to consider when building each tree.
 Using a fraction (e.g.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $0.8$
\end_inset

) introduces randomness,
 which helps the model avoid focusing too heavily on a single feature and improves generalization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
MaximumIterations
\series default
 (Iterations):
 The number of times the algorithm passes through the entire training dataset to update weights.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
MaxBinCount
\series default
:
 Sets the maximum number of bins used to group features,
 helping to discretize continuous variables for more efficient processing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Clusters
\series default
 (K-Means):
 Defines the number of centroids (hidden neurons) used to represent the input space.
 Each cluster captures a local region of the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Sigma
\series default
 (Gaussian Spread):
 Controls the width of the radial basis function (Gaussian kernel).
 It determines how far the influence of a single neuron extends in the input space;
 a smaller sigma makes neurons more sensitive to localized inputs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
L2Regularization
\series default
:
 A penalty applied to the magnitude of the model weights.
 It discourages overly large weights,
 which helps in preventing overfitting and improves the model's ability to generalize to unseen data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Tolerance
\series default
:
 The threshold for the optimization algorithm to determine convergence.
 It dictates how small the improvements in the loss function must be before the algorithm stops.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Experimental results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Definition of the two binary problems.
 Before presenting the numerical results,
 it is important to clarify how the target variable was constructed for each market.
 The original dataset label (Label ∈ {1,
 X,
 2}) corresponds to the three-way outcome of a match (home win,
 draw,
 away win).
 For the purposes of this analysis,
 the problem was not treated as a three-class classification task,
 but was instead reformulated into two separate binary classification problems,
 one for each betting market:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Home Win market (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Home-win-experimental"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

):
 The positive class corresponds to the outcome "1" (home win),
 while the classes "X" (draw) and "2" (away win) were merged into a single negative class,
 "not Home Win." In other words,
 the model is asked to answer the question "will the home team win or not," collapsing the distinction between a draw and an away win.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Away Win market (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Away-win-experimental"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

):
 Conversely,
 the positive class corresponds to the outcome "2" (away win),
 while the classes "X" (draw) and "1" (home win) were merged into a single negative class,
 "not Away Win."
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This approach was chosen because it directly mirrors how real-world betting markets of the "Double Chance" / single-market type are structured (e.g.,
 "1" versus "X2," and "2" versus "1X"),
 and it allows each model to be independently optimized for the specific market of interest,
 without the constraints of a shared multi-class loss function.
 It is worth noting that this merging directly affects class balance:
 in the Away Win market,
 the positive class ("2") is the minority category in most leagues,
 which partly explains the systematically lower Recall values observed both in Table 4 and in the per-league stability analysis (§3.3,
 Figures 1–3).
\end_layout

